                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I've had this game since 2012, from one of the early Humble Bundles from back in the day, and although I'd made an attempt at playing it back then, I quickly abandoned it in favour of whatever else I happened to be playing at the time. Only this year did I get back to it, and after finishing it, I feel it's time to write a short review for it.\n\nCrayon Physics Deluxe is, basically, a casual puzzle game, although the \"casual\" part should go in huge quotation marks, as some of the levels can be pretty challenging, either in terms of figuring out what to do, or actually doing it. See, the game has pretty decent physics simulation for an indie game made on a budget in 2009, and the \"draw your solutions\" mechanic is quite engaging, but still, that very same mechanic makes it frustrating to play at times. I'll admit to using a guide for some of the more challenging puzzles, mostly for the \"Elegant\" solutions, and even when following the guide pretty closely, it took me lots of trial and error to actually get the solution to work.\n\nStill, as frustrating as it was, it was also challenging, in the sense that I'd keep resetting the level in the hopes that this was the time I got the solution just right, and when I did... It was a great feeling. I'd also realize I'd spent the last half hour trying to solve one puzzle; even then, I didn't feel like I'd wasted my time. I think it helps that once you set things in motion, it takes very little time to see whether your solution will work or not.\n\nSpeaking of solutions, I already mentioned the \"Elegant\" solutions above. That is one of three types of solution you can get; the other two are the \"Old School\" and \"Awesome\" solutions. While the game explains what the requirements for each are, I'll also mention them here: Elegant solutions only allow you to draw one object (though you can also draw pins and ropes), Old School doesn't let you draw pins and ropes, and the Awesome solution, well, you pick which one you want, as the game saves all your solutions. Also, the first two solutions don't let you touch the ball.\n\nIn terms of graphics, well, it's evocative of crayon drawings you'd expect from a small child, so it all looks hand-drawn (as will your solutions); what's funny is you can even draw on the game map! It's pretty fun in that regard, as you can mess around and personalize it to a certain degree. The soundtrack is also very simple, but it sets a relaxing mood.\n\nAll in all, a decent game, so it gets a thumbs up from me. Recommended.",
